{
  "analysis": "I'm in the dclm repo. There's a commit 'Add fake git and hf tokens' suggesting fake tokens were intentionally added for this sanitization task. The git status shows files staged as deleted, but they still exist on disk. My main goal is to find and replace all API keys/tokens with placeholders. I need to search the working tree files for sensitive values.",
  "plan": "I'll search the repository (excluding .git) for common secret patterns: AWS keys, Huggingface tokens, generic API keys, tokens, passwords, etc. I'll use grep with patterns and review actual values to replace them consistently with placeholders. Let me start with broad searches.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -rniE 'hf_|huggingface|access_key|secret|token|api[_-]?key|password|passwd|bearer|authorization' --include='*' . --exclude-dir=.git 2>/dev/null | grep -viE 'node_modules' | head -80\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I found the sensitive values. In `ray_processing/process.py` and `ray_processing/ray_cluster.yaml` there are:\n- AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: `AKIA1234567890123456`\n- AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: `D4w8z9wKN1aVeT3BpQj6kIuN7wH8X0M9KfV5OqzF`\n- Huggingface token: `hf_abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz123456`\n\nThe grep matched many non-secret 'token' references (code tokens, tokenizer). I need to confirm ALL occurrences of the actual secret strings and check for any other secrets before replacing. Let me search for each secret value specifically and look for other patterns like private keys, generic tokens/env vars.",
  "plan": "I'll do targeted searches for the exact secret strings to find every occurrence. I'll also search for other common secret patterns (private keys, tokens in env/yml files, etc.) to be thorough. Then I'll replace each with consistent placeholders.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -rn 'AKIA1234567890123456\\|D4w8z9wKN1aVeT3BpQj6kIuN7wH8X0M9KfV5OqzF\\|hf_abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz123456' . --exclude-dir=.git\n",
      "duration": 0.3
